---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Layout title="Which line actually runs the SQL?" description="Practice card. Five lines of ActiveRecord chaining, only one of them talks to the database. Drills Relation laziness.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Reading the Source · Card 2</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">Which line actually runs the SQL?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">Five lines of <code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-base">User.where(...)</code> chaining. Only one of them hits the database. Pick it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-gray-500">🔍</span> The code
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">Reading top to bottom, which line is the moment the database wakes up?</p>
          <div class="border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-gray-50/40">
            <CodeBlock code={`def call
  users = User.where(active: true)        # line 1
  users = users.where(role: "admin")       # line 2
  users = users.order(:created_at).limit(10) # line 3
  Rails.logger.info "Found admins"         # line 4
  users.each { |u| send_email(u) }         # line 5
end`} language="ruby" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The question</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Which of the five lines is the first one to execute SQL against the database? And what kind of methods, in general, force a Relation to run?</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <div id="prompt-section" class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-7">
            <p class="text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Take a moment.</strong> A Relation is a description of a query. It stays a description until someone asks it for actual data. Pick the line that asks.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="pt-2">
            <button
              id="show-solution-btn"
              class="w-full px-6 py-3 bg-ruby text-white rounded-lg hover:bg-ruby-dark transition-colors font-medium"
            >
              Reveal the answer →
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="solution-section" class="hidden space-y-6">
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-green-600">✅</span> The answer
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Line 5.</strong> Lines 1-3 all return new Relation objects without touching the database. Each <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.where</code> / <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.order</code> / <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.limit</code> adds to the spec. Line 4 doesn't even mention the relation. Line 5 calls <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.each</code>, which forces the Relation to load. That's when the SQL fires.</p>
            <div class="border-2 border-green-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-green-50/30">
              <CodeBlock code={`# At line 5, Rails finally runs:
#
#   SELECT "users".* FROM "users"
#   WHERE "users"."active" = TRUE
#     AND "users"."role" = 'admin'
#   ORDER BY "users"."created_at" ASC
#   LIMIT 10

# The result is cached on the relation, so a second
# .each call would NOT re-run the SQL.`} language="ruby" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-lg p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>💡</span> Which methods force the SQL to run?
            </h3>
            <div class="space-y-3 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              <p>Any method that needs the actual rows: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.each</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.to_a</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.first</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.last</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.count</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.find_each</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.pluck</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.exists?</code>, or interpolating into a view (which calls <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.to_a</code>).</p>
              <p>Chain methods (<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.where</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.order</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.limit</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.includes</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.joins</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.select</code>) return new Relations and don't run SQL. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.to_sql</code> doesn't run SQL either; it returns the string Rails would send.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>📚</span> Theory
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">For the full walkthrough of how a query becomes SQL, read <a href="/lessons/source-activerecord-query" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark font-medium">Card 3 of Reading the Source</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-belongs-to-optional" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 1</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-require-vs-bracket" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 3 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <script>
    const btn = document.getElementById('show-solution-btn');
    const prompt = document.getElementById('prompt-section');
    const solution = document.getElementById('solution-section');
    if (btn && prompt && solution) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        prompt.classList.add('hidden');
        solution.classList.remove('hidden');
        if (window.innerWidth < 1024) {
          solution.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
        }
      });
    }
  </script>
</Layout>
